  "body": "Recently I decided to understand how do actually timers work. I went to read whatwg spec and [came across onto following step with text](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/timers-and-user-prompts.html#run-steps-after-a-timeout):\r\n> If global is a [Window](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/window-object.html#window) object, wait until global's [associated Document](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/window-object.html#concept-document-window) has been [fully active](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#fully-active) for a further milliseconds milliseconds (not necessarily consecutively).\r\n>\r\n> Otherwise, global is a [WorkerGlobalScope](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/workers.html#workerglobalscope) object; wait until milliseconds milliseconds have passed with the worker not suspended (not necessarily consecutively).\r\n\r\nWhat does it mean? What are we waiting for? When we are calling setTimeout, can the associated Document is not ready yet? Moreover what does `not necessarily consecutively` (in this context) also mean?",
